\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Changing the signs of the spatial squares}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For #{n=2m\ge2} choose a time direction #{t}. Use the
even volume grading #{C=\ui^m\psI} of \ref{fdip-009X}, and
a \vocabk{charge conjugation of sign}{fdip-017H} #{c_\eta}.
Set #{\gamma^0=\varepsilon_t} and #{\gamma^j=\ui\varepsilon_j}
for spatial indices #{j\ne t}, with #{0} labeling the time
generator. Each spatial square is now #{-\bbI}, while the
time square is #{\bbI}. The antiunitary #{(C\varepsilon_t)c_\eta}
conjugates every one of these generators with sign #{-\eta}
and conjugates #{C} with sign #{(-1)^{m+1}}.}

\p{On fields extend both antiunitaries sitewise with the same
fiber antiunitary #{c_F}. Compose on the right with the unitary
#{\mathcal P=(\varepsilon_t\otimes\bbI_F)h^*} of
\ref{fdip-013T}, where #{h} fixes coordinate #{t} and negates
the others. The field composite with #{(C\varepsilon_t)c_\eta}
conjugates #{C} with sign #{(-1)^m}. The composite
#{\mathcal Q\mathcal P} with #{\mathcal Q=c_\eta\otimes c_F}
conjugates #{C} with sign #{(-1)^{m+1}}.
Here #{C} acts on spin at every site.}

\proof{
\p{The original directions square to the identity. Multiplying
a spatial direction by #{\ui} reverses its square as in
\ref{fdip-00LZ}. Conjugate linearity gives #{c_\eta}
sign #{\eta} on #{\gamma^0} and #{-\eta} on each
#{\gamma^j}. Conjugation by #{\varepsilon_t} fixes the time
direction and negates every spatial one. Conjugation by #{C}
negates all of them. Hence their product #{C\varepsilon_t}
negates the time generator and fixes the spatial generators.
The resulting uniform sign is #{-\eta}.}

\p{The original antiunitary gives #{(-1)^m} on #{C}
by \ref{fdip-017P}, while #{C\varepsilon_t} anticommutes
with #{C}. This gives #{(-1)^{m+1}}. Site pullback fixes the
constant spin grading, and the value factor of #{\mathcal P}
negates it. Composing on the right with #{\mathcal P}
therefore changes the sign once more in each case.}
}
